# Multivariate polynomial normal forms over Z -- the data layer of the
# polynomial normal-form module. Pure forward-chaining rules; covers
# addition, negation, subtraction and multiplication. The
# term-to-polynomial compiler (topoly) is a separate follow-up module.
#
# The module is independent of symbolic-core: it is a DATA layer, like
# the arithmetic modules. The compiler bridging symbolic terms to this
# layer is a separate follow-up module.
#
# REPRESENTATION (recursive dense, collapsed, variable-tagged):
#   - A CONSTANT polynomial is a zint term: (pos zint N) / (neg zint N).
#   - A NON-CONSTANT polynomial with main variable V is (V poly L),
#     where L is a cons list of canonical polynomials, LSB-first: the
#     V^0 coefficient is the outermost cell -- the numeral orientation,
#     because a number in base b IS a polynomial evaluated at b, and
#     the rules below are the digit recursions with the carry removed.
#   - COLLAPSE: a polynomial constant in V is represented by its
#     constant coefficient directly, never as (V poly (C cons nil)).
#     Canonical coefficient lists therefore have length >= 2.
#   - The LAST list element (leading coefficient) is never the zero
#     polynomial; inner zeros are value-relevant and kept. By collapse,
#     the zero polynomial at EVERY level is the single node
#     (pos zint &0) -- which is what makes the strip stage's zero test
#     a pattern match instead of a structural recursion.
#   - NESTING ORDER: composite coefficients inside (V poly L) have main
#     variables strictly inner to V. The order is declared by the user
#     as pouter facts (V pouter W: V is strictly outer); adjacent pairs
#     suffice, transitivity is provided below. The order must be a
#     STRICT total order on the variables in use -- declaring both
#     V pouter W and W pouter V would break single-valuedness, exactly
#     like conflicting declared equations in symbolic-core.
#   - The variable tag resolves the ambiguity a collapsed UNTAGGED list
#     representation would have across nesting levels (the list for y
#     as a constant-in-x polynomial would be node-identical to the list
#     for x); with the tag, every canonical polynomial is a unique
#     hash-consed node, so equality of normal forms is node identity.
#
# OPERATIONS (results exposed under =, the uniform query idiom):
#   (P padd Q) = R        addition
#   (:pneg P) = R         negation (request self-fact, testprime idiom)
#   (P psub Q) = R        subtraction, via pneg + padd
#   (P pmul Q) = R        multiplication
#
# CANONICITY: all rules yield canonical results for canonical operands.
# Only same-main-variable addition can produce non-canonical raw lists
# (cancellation of present coefficients, possibly including the leading
# one); its results run through the strip/collapse stage below -- the
# canonnum pattern with the zero POLYNOMIAL in place of the zero digit.
# Head-adjustment cases (different main variables, or constant into
# composite) touch only the V^0 coefficient: the tail -- including the
# nonzero leading coefficient -- passes through unchanged, and a zero
# HEAD is an inner zero, which is canonical. Negation maps nonzero
# coefficients to nonzero coefficients elementwise. Multiplication
# needs no strip stage of its own: see the pmul section. Applying
# an operation to non-polynomial or non-canonical operands derives
# nothing -- partiality by absence, as everywhere in the stdlib.
#
# ZERO TEST, POSITIVELY: the strip rules must distinguish zero from
# nonzero elements. Zero is matched directly as the ground pattern
# (pos zint &0). Nonzero is derived as a positive marker (pnonzero)
# from the three positive shapes -- (neg zint N), (pos zint N) with
# N > &0 via an in-system cmp, and (W poly K) composites (never zero
# by the leading-coefficient invariant). This keeps the module fully
# inside the positive stratum: no negation, no != against structured
# nodes.

# Exclude operators with possible operand value coincidences from the
# self-fact display sugar ((P padd P) is x + x, not a request marker,
# and elementwise scaling seeds products like ((pos zint &2) pmul
# (pos zint &2))). The marker predicates (pneg, lneg, needsplcanon,
# plcanon, pzcheck, pnonzero) stay unregistered on purpose: they are
# genuine self-facts. lscale/lprod need no entry -- a scalar and a
# cons list are never the same node.

.import integer-arithmetic

%(zelph/no-selffact-sugar "padd" "psub" "ladd" "pmul")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variable order: adjacent declarations suffice
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(A pouter B, B pouter C) => (A pouter C)

# ===========================================================================
# Addition (P padd Q) -- flat case analysis on the operand shapes (the
# integer-arithmetic design), recursing through coefficient pairs.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules PZ -- constant + constant: delegate to the Z facade (an ordinary
# + fact, routed to z+, result under =). Canonical: z+ results are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((G zint A) padd (H zint B)) => ((G zint A) + (H zint B))
((G zint A) padd (H zint B), ((G zint A) + (H zint B)) = R)
=> (((G zint A) padd (H zint B)) = R)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules PS -- same main variable: element-wise list addition (the digit
# recursion minus the carry). Seeds the internal ladd state; the
# connect stage below consumes its canonicalized result.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((V poly L) padd (V poly M)) => (L ladd M)

# ladd recursion: seed the element addition, recurse on the tails.
((A cons R) ladd (B cons S)) => (A padd B)
((A cons R) ladd (B cons S)) => (R ladd S)

((A cons R) ladd (B cons S),
 (A padd B) = D,
 (R ladd S) lsum T)
=> (((A cons R) ladd (B cons S)) lsum (D cons T))

# Short circuits: adding the empty list is the identity -- the
# zero-extension of D2/D3 without the digit table.
((A cons R) ladd nil) => (((A cons R) ladd nil) lsum (A cons R))
(nil ladd (B cons S)) => ((nil ladd (B cons S)) lsum (B cons S))
(nil ladd nil) => ((nil ladd nil) lsum nil)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules PH -- head adjustment: the inner operand is constant relative
# to the outer one and adds into its V^0 coefficient. The tail R passes
# through unchanged (canonical lists have length >= 2, so R != nil, and
# its last element stays the untouched nonzero leading coefficient); a
# zero head is an inner zero and therefore canonical as-is.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# composite + inner composite, both orientations
((V poly (A cons R)) padd (W poly M), V pouter W) => (A padd (W poly M))
((V poly (A cons R)) padd (W poly M), V pouter W,
 (A padd (W poly M)) = D)
=> (((V poly (A cons R)) padd (W poly M)) = (V poly (D cons R)))

((W poly M) padd (V poly (A cons R)), V pouter W) => ((W poly M) padd A)
((W poly M) padd (V poly (A cons R)), V pouter W,
 ((W poly M) padd A) = D)
=> (((W poly M) padd (V poly (A cons R))) = (V poly (D cons R)))

# constant + composite, both orientations
((G zint N) padd (V poly (A cons R))) => ((G zint N) padd A)
((G zint N) padd (V poly (A cons R)), ((G zint N) padd A) = D)
=> (((G zint N) padd (V poly (A cons R))) = (V poly (D cons R)))

((V poly (A cons R)) padd (G zint N)) => (A padd (G zint N))
((V poly (A cons R)) padd (G zint N), (A padd (G zint N)) = D)
=> (((V poly (A cons R)) padd (G zint N)) = (V poly (D cons R)))

# ===========================================================================
# Canonicalization of same-main-variable sums -- the canonnum pattern
# with the zero polynomial (pos zint &0) in place of the zero digit.
# Only lsum results enter (KT-analog); the marker recursion walks the
# suffixes; strip drops leading zeros (innermost cells, next to nil).
# ===========================================================================

# Trigger and decompose (guarded like KD1: no nil marker state)
((L ladd M) lsum T) => (T needsplcanon T)
((A cons R) needsplcanon (A cons R), R != nil) => (R needsplcanon R)

# Element zero check: derive the positive nonzero marker
((A cons R) needsplcanon (A cons R)) => (A pzcheck A)

((neg zint N) pzcheck (neg zint N))
=> ((neg zint N) pnonzero (neg zint N))
((pos zint N) pzcheck (pos zint N)) => (N cmp &0)
((pos zint N) pzcheck (pos zint N), N > &0)
=> ((pos zint N) pnonzero (pos zint N))
((W poly K) pzcheck (W poly K))
=> ((W poly K) pnonzero (W poly K))

# Strip rules (KA1-KA5 analogs; tail semantics -- may reach nil)
((A cons nil) needsplcanon (A cons nil), A pnonzero A)
=> ((A cons nil) plcanon (A cons nil))

(((pos zint &0) cons nil) needsplcanon ((pos zint &0) cons nil))
=> (((pos zint &0) cons nil) plcanon nil)

((A cons R) needsplcanon (A cons R), A pnonzero A, R != nil,
 R plcanon S)
=> ((A cons R) plcanon (A cons S))

(((pos zint &0) cons R) needsplcanon ((pos zint &0) cons R), R != nil,
 R plcanon S, S != nil)
=> (((pos zint &0) cons R) plcanon ((pos zint &0) cons S))

(((pos zint &0) cons R) needsplcanon ((pos zint &0) cons R), R != nil,
 R plcanon nil)
=> (((pos zint &0) cons R) plcanon nil)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules PSC -- connect the same-main-variable case: total cancellation
# yields the zero polynomial, a single surviving cell collapses to its
# coefficient, longer lists keep the variable tag. The three plcanon
# shapes are mutually exclusive, so = stays single-valued.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((V poly L) padd (V poly M), (L ladd M) lsum T, T plcanon nil)
=> (((V poly L) padd (V poly M)) = (pos zint &0))

((V poly L) padd (V poly M), (L ladd M) lsum T, T plcanon (A cons nil))
=> (((V poly L) padd (V poly M)) = A)

((V poly L) padd (V poly M), (L ladd M) lsum T,
 T plcanon (A cons (B cons S)))
=> (((V poly L) padd (V poly M)) = (V poly (A cons (B cons S))))

# ===========================================================================
# Negation (:pneg P) -- elementwise; nonzero maps to nonzero, so the
# result is canonical without any strip stage. The zint cases mirror
# the ZN rules of symbolic-integers ((neg zint &0) is never produced).
# ===========================================================================
((pos zint N) pneg (pos zint N)) => (N cmp &0)
((pos zint N) pneg (pos zint N), N > &0)
=> (((pos zint N) pneg (pos zint N)) = (neg zint N))
((pos zint &0) pneg (pos zint &0))
=> (((pos zint &0) pneg (pos zint &0)) = (pos zint &0))
((neg zint N) pneg (neg zint N))
=> (((neg zint N) pneg (neg zint N)) = (pos zint N))

((V poly L) pneg (V poly L)) => (L lneg L)

((A cons R) lneg (A cons R)) => (A pneg A)
((A cons R) lneg (A cons R), R != nil) => (R lneg R)

((A cons nil) lneg (A cons nil), (A pneg A) = D)
=> (((A cons nil) lneg (A cons nil)) lnegres (D cons nil))

((A cons R) lneg (A cons R), R != nil,
 (A pneg A) = D,
 (R lneg R) lnegres T)
=> (((A cons R) lneg (A cons R)) lnegres (D cons T))

((V poly L) pneg (V poly L), (L lneg L) lnegres T)
=> (((V poly L) pneg (V poly L)) = (V poly T))

# ===========================================================================
# Subtraction (P psub Q) = P padd (-Q) -- pure delegation.
# ===========================================================================
(P psub Q) => (Q pneg Q)
(P psub Q, (Q pneg Q) = N) => (P padd N)
(P psub Q, (Q pneg Q) = N, (P padd N) = R) => ((P psub Q) = R)

# ===========================================================================
# Multiplication (P pmul Q) -- flat case analysis on the operand
# shapes, like padd. No strip stage of its own: over Z (an integral
# domain) products of nonzero coefficients are nonzero, so scaling
# preserves canonicity elementwise, and the only accumulation (the
# schoolbook case) delegates to padd, which owns its canonicalization.
# The cases are mutually exclusive, so = stays single-valued: constant
# x constant is the Z facade; the ground zero times a composite has
# direct rules; a NONZERO constant or an inner-variable composite
# scales elementwise; same-main-variable composites run the schoolbook
# recursion. Two composites without a declared pouter order derive
# nothing -- partiality by absence.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules MZ -- constant x constant: delegate to the Z facade (an
# ordinary * fact, routed to zx; zero operands are canonical there).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((G zint A) pmul (H zint B)) => ((G zint A) * (H zint B))
((G zint A) pmul (H zint B), ((G zint A) * (H zint B)) = R)
=> (((G zint A) pmul (H zint B)) = R)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules M0 -- the zero polynomial absorbs composites, both
# orientations. Scaling by zero would build an all-zero list
# (non-canonical), so the zero scalar is excluded from the scaling
# case via the pnonzero guard; these ground rules answer it directly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((pos zint &0) pmul (V poly L))
=> (((pos zint &0) pmul (V poly L)) = (pos zint &0))
((V poly L) pmul (pos zint &0))
=> (((V poly L) pmul (pos zint &0)) = (pos zint &0))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules MS -- scaling: a nonzero constant or an inner-variable
# composite times a coefficient list, elementwise. Both operand
# orientations seed the SAME internal (C lscale L) state and read the
# same lprod result, so commutative orders connect to the identical
# result node by construction. Canonicity without a strip stage:
# scaling preserves the list length, the nonzero leading coefficient
# stays nonzero, and inner zeros scale to the zero polynomial node
# (via rules MZ/M0). The zint scalar's nonzero guard reuses the
# pzcheck/pnonzero markers of the canonicalization stage above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# zint scalar, both orientations
((G zint N) pmul (V poly L)) => ((G zint N) pzcheck (G zint N))
((V poly L) pmul (G zint N)) => ((G zint N) pzcheck (G zint N))

((G zint N) pmul (V poly L), (G zint N) pnonzero (G zint N))
=> ((G zint N) lscale L)
((V poly L) pmul (G zint N), (G zint N) pnonzero (G zint N))
=> ((G zint N) lscale L)

((G zint N) pmul (V poly L), ((G zint N) lscale L) lprod S)
=> (((G zint N) pmul (V poly L)) = (V poly S))
((V poly L) pmul (G zint N), ((G zint N) lscale L) lprod S)
=> (((V poly L) pmul (G zint N)) = (V poly S))

# inner-variable composite scalar, both orientations (composites are
# never zero by the leading-coefficient invariant: no guard needed)
((V poly L) pmul (W poly M), V pouter W) => ((W poly M) lscale L)
((W poly M) pmul (V poly L), V pouter W) => ((W poly M) lscale L)

((V poly L) pmul (W poly M), V pouter W, ((W poly M) lscale L) lprod S)
=> (((V poly L) pmul (W poly M)) = (V poly S))
((W poly M) pmul (V poly L), V pouter W, ((W poly M) lscale L) lprod S)
=> (((W poly M) pmul (V poly L)) = (V poly S))

# lscale recursion: seed the elementwise product (fixed orientation,
# scalar first -- the connected result node is orientation-independent
# anyway, since elementwise results are canonical forms), recurse on
# the tail.
(C lscale (A cons R)) => (C pmul A)
(C lscale (A cons R), R != nil) => (C lscale R)

(C lscale (A cons nil), (C pmul A) = D)
=> ((C lscale (A cons nil)) lprod (D cons nil))

(C lscale (A cons R), R != nil,
 (C pmul A) = D,
 (C lscale R) lprod T)
=> ((C lscale (A cons R)) lprod (D cons T))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules MM -- same main variable: schoolbook recursion on the left
# coefficient list,  (A cons R) x Q  =  A*Q  padd  V*(R x Q).
# Head and tail products are seeded as ordinary pmul facts: the head
# coefficient A is constant or strictly inner relative to V (the
# scaling cases above); the tail polynomial is B for the collapsed
# one-cell tail, (V poly R) otherwise (a canonical suffix: its last
# cell is the operand's untouched nonzero leading coefficient).
#
# The tail product ALWAYS has main variable V: Q = (V poly M) has
# V-degree >= 1 (canonical lists have length >= 2) and the tail
# polynomial is nonzero, so their product has V-degree >= 1 over an
# integral domain. The connect conditions match that shape,
# (V poly K) -- if the invariant were ever violated they would fall
# silent instead of assembling garbage. The V-shift is then the free
# cons: prepending the zero polynomial as the new V^0 coefficient --
# an inner zero, canonical, and the shifted list keeps its nonzero
# last cell. Accumulation delegates to padd (MA1/MA2 pattern).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((V poly (A cons R)) pmul (V poly M)) => (A pmul (V poly M))

((V poly (A cons (B cons nil))) pmul (V poly M)) => (B pmul (V poly M))
((V poly (A cons (B cons (C cons S)))) pmul (V poly M))
=> ((V poly (B cons (C cons S))) pmul (V poly M))

# one-cell tail: assemble and connect
((V poly (A cons (B cons nil))) pmul (V poly M),
 (A pmul (V poly M)) = H,
 (B pmul (V poly M)) = (V poly K))
=> (H padd (V poly ((pos zint &0) cons K)))

((V poly (A cons (B cons nil))) pmul (V poly M),
 (A pmul (V poly M)) = H,
 (B pmul (V poly M)) = (V poly K),
 (H padd (V poly ((pos zint &0) cons K))) = R)
=> (((V poly (A cons (B cons nil))) pmul (V poly M)) = R)

# longer tail: assemble and connect via the recursive product
((V poly (A cons (B cons (C cons S)))) pmul (V poly M),
 (A pmul (V poly M)) = H,
 ((V poly (B cons (C cons S))) pmul (V poly M)) = (V poly K))
=> (H padd (V poly ((pos zint &0) cons K)))

((V poly (A cons (B cons (C cons S)))) pmul (V poly M),
 (A pmul (V poly M)) = H,
 ((V poly (B cons (C cons S))) pmul (V poly M)) = (V poly K),
 (H padd (V poly ((pos zint &0) cons K))) = R)
=> (((V poly (A cons (B cons (C cons S)))) pmul (V poly M)) = R)
